{
  "schema": "embed-layout",
  "protocol_version": "v0.1",
  "spec_section": "wikilink.md §2 form 12 / §5.1; markdown-dialect.md §6 / §7",
  "description": "Pins the form-C embed layout segments (myne-md/myne#409): `![[att:<id>|filename|w=300|align=center|alt=free text to the end]]`. Two implementations must agree and neither can detect drift alone: the canonical grammar is TypeScript in `@myne/dialect/embedLayout` (source-consumed by desktop, web and mobile), the export transform is Rust in `myne-core`'s `rewrite_embeds_to_final_names`: so this category is what holds them together. It pins four rules that each exist because of a specific failure: segment 1 is ALWAYS the filename and an unkeyed later segment rejoins it (an attachment filename may already contain a pipe, so a positional or shape-recognised grammar would silently reinterpret notes already on disk); `alt=` is terminal (alt prose contains pipes, and the importer carries markdown-image alt straight into this slot); a KNOWN key with an invalid value is dropped AND reported while an UNRECOGNISED key is dropped SILENTLY (version skew across independently-versioned clients reading one synced vault is not an error the reader can act on); and the export translates only a pixel `w=` into Obsidian's own spelling, passing everything else through opaquely rather than deleting bytes it failed to understand. Deterministic cross-client behavior in the property-coercion / markdown-comment class: not a codec, not cryptographic. Hand-curated; a failing vector in any port is a stop-the-world bug.",
  "cases": [
